L000157	\subsection{Derivation of Magnon Path Integral}
L000158	
L000159	In this subsection we will re-derive the dissipative single magnon evolution of the main text (Eq.~\eqref{nhh}) from a more microscopic perspective and analytically bound the correlation function under a number of simplifying assumptions. We begin with the standard correlation function in the infinite temperature state
L000160	\begin{equation}\label{corr}
L000161	    \langle \sigma^{-}_{x_{t}}\l t\r\sigma^{+}_{x_{0}}\l 0 \r \rangle = \sum_{\psi \in \mc{H}_{\rm V}} p\l \psi \r\bra{\psi} \sigma^{-}_{x_{t}}\l t\r\sigma^{+}_{x_{0}}\l 0 \r \ket{\psi} + \sum_{\psi\in \mc{H} \setminus \mc{H}_{\rm V}}   p \l \psi \r \bra{\psi}\sigma^{-}_{x_{t}}\l t\r\sigma^{+}_{x_{0}}\l 0 \r \ket{\psi}
L000162	\end{equation}
L000163	where we have separated out states $\ket{\psi}\in \mc{H}_{\rm V}$ which contain a void of size $\ell$ around the raising/lowering operator, from those that do not. The void size $\ell$ will be later be optimized to find the dominant contribution to the correlation function. We now conjecture that the second term is subleading, and ignore it henceforth.
L000164	
L000165	
L000166	In general, one could split up the time evolution into a path sum by inserting a complete set of states at each time step. We instead insert an \emph{incomplete} set of states---those consisting of a single magnon above the instantaneous ``background'' state that would obtain if $\ket{\psi}$ had been allowed to evolve without any perturbation. The histories that are left out of this prescription are those in which the magnon interacts nontrivially with the background; such histories do not contribute to local correlation functions. This no-backflow assumption \cite{von_Keyserlingk_2022} neglects autocorrelation of the background density. Following these steps, we arrive at the following ``path integral'' representation of the correlation function:
L000167	\begin{equation}
L000168	    \bra{\psi} \sigma^{-}_{x_{t}}\l t\r\sigma^{+}_{x_{0}}\l 0 \r \ket{\psi}
L000169	    = \bra{\psi \l t \r} \sigma^{-}_{x_{t}} U^{t}\sigma^{+}_{x_{0}}\ket{\psi\l 0 \r}
L000170	    \approx \bra{\psi \l t \r} \sigma^{-}_{x_{t}} \prod_{\tau : \, t-1 \leftarrow 1  } \l U  \sum_{x_{\tau}}   \sigma^{+}_{x_{\tau}}\ket{\psi\l \tau \r} \bra{ \psi\l \tau\r} \sigma^{-}_{x_{\tau}}\r  U \sigma^{+}_{x_{0}}\ket{\psi}.
L000171	\end{equation}
L000172	
L000173	We can reorganize this path integral by evaluating the single-timestep, state-dependent propagator from $x$ to $y$:
L000174	\begin{eqnarray}
L000175	    &&\bra{\psi \l  \tau \r} \sigma^{-}_{y}U\sigma^{+}_{x}\ket{\psi\l \tau - 1\r}  = \bra{\psi \l  \tau \r} \sigma^{-}_{y}U\sigma^{+}_{x}U^{\dagger}\ket{\psi\l \tau \r} \nonumber \\
L000176	     & &\ \ \approx e^{- \gamma \bra{\psi\l \tau \r}\hat{n}\l \frac{x+y}{2}\r\ket{\psi\l\tau\r} } G_{0}\l x, y \r \braket{\psi\l\tau \r} = e^{- \gamma \bra{\psi\l \tau \r} \hat{n}\l \frac{x+y}{2}\r\ket{\psi\l\tau\r} } G_{0}\l x, y \r
L000177	\end{eqnarray}
L000178	where $G_{0}\l x, y \r = \bra{y} e^{-i\omega\l \hat{p}\r}\ket{x}$ is the free magnon propagator and $\gamma$ is a model dependent parameter.
L000179	
L000180	To approximate this expectation value, we adopt a mean field assumption: set $n(x,t) \equiv \bra{\psi(t)} \hat{n}(x)\ket{\psi(t)}$, where $n\l x, t\r$ is a classical ``background'' density field over which the magnon will evolve.
L000181	
L000182	
L000183	
L000184	In the continuous-time limit, $\hat U_{n\l t \r}$ is the propagator associated with the annealed non-Hermitian Hamiltonian in Eq.~\eqref{nhh} of the main text (after reinstating time-dependent couplings which are periodic in the Floquet period).
L000185	
L000186	
L000187	The mean density approximation allows us to analytically lower bound the survival probability of the magnon and obtain an estimate on the size of the correlation function using the annealed single-body magnon propagator $\hat{U}_{n(\tau)} = \sum_{x,y}\ket{y}e^{-\gamma n\l \frac{x+y}{2}, \tau\r} G_{0}\l x, y \r \bra{x}$. In doing so, we approximate the correlation function in states $\psi$ with a void of length $\ell$ around the raising/lowering operators as $\langle \sigma^{-}_{y}\l t\r\sigma^{+}_{x}\l 0 \r \rangle \sim p_{\ell} \bra{y} \prod_{\tau: t \leftarrow 1 } \hat{U}_{n\l \tau \r} \ket{x}$
L000188	
L000189	
L000190	
L000191	
L000192	where $p_{\ell} \sim e^{- \mc{O}\l \ell\r}$ is the probability of finding such a void. Using the above equation we can rewrite the norm-squared correlator (summed over positions) as
L000193	\begin{equation}
L000194	    \ell^{-1}\sum_{x,y} \lv \langle\sigma^{-}_{y}\l t \r \sigma^{+}_{x}\rangle\rv^{2} \sim \ell^{-1} p_{\ell}\sum_{x,y} \Big| \bra{y}\prod_{\tau: t \leftarrow 1 } \hat{U}_{n\l \tau \r} \ket{x}\Big|^{2} > \ell^{-1}p_{\ell}\sum_{x,y} \Big| \bra{y} \l \hat{U}_{n(t)}\r^{t}\ket{x}\Big|^{2} ,
L000195	    \label{eqn:bound_start}
L000196	\end{equation}
L000197	where in the last inequality we have assumed the density at all times to be the same as the density at late times, overestimating dissipative effects. In the next section we will lower bound the right hand side of Eq.~\eqref{eqn:bound_start}, accounting for the magnon's velocity and kinetic energy; as a consequence we will also control subleading terms in the density profile in a perturbation series in $x/\ell$ where $x$ is the center of the void (the local minimum of the background density).
